/**
@defgroup api_reference Public API
@brief Everything reachable from `#include <batchlas.hh>`, plus the two opt-in SYCL headers.

Every entry point enqueues on a batchlas::Queue and returns a batchlas::Event;
nothing is readable until the queue (or the event) is waited on. The backend is
taken from the Queue, options are designated-initialiser structs, and scratch
comes from the Queue's workspace arena. The conventions shared by all entry
points are in @ref guide_cpp_api.

@{

@defgroup api_core Queues, devices and events
@brief batchlas::Queue, batchlas::Device, batchlas::Event and SYCL interop.

String conversions and other helpers: @ref api_core_lowlevel.

@defgroup api_matrix Matrices, views and vectors
@brief Column-major batched containers and the non-owning views every entry point takes.

String conversions and other helpers: @ref api_matrix_lowlevel.

@defgroup api_enums Enumerations and scalar concepts
@brief Transpose, Uplo, Side, Diag, Backend and the scalar concepts that constrain every template.

String conversions and other helpers: @ref api_enums_lowlevel.

@defgroup api_options Option structs
@brief Designated-initialiser option structs accepted by the entry points.

@defgroup api_blas2 BLAS level 2
@brief Batched matrix-vector operations.

@defgroup api_blas3 BLAS level 3
@brief Batched matrix-matrix operations: gemm, trsm, trmm, symm/hemm, syrk/herk, syr2k/her2k.

Lower-level functions (algorithm-specific variants, workspace queries, validation and helpers): @ref api_blas3_lowlevel.

@defgroup api_factorizations Factorizations and linear solvers
@brief Cholesky and LU factorizations, their solves and inverses.

Lower-level functions (algorithm-specific variants, workspace queries, validation and helpers): @ref api_factorizations_lowlevel.

@defgroup api_qr QR and orthogonal transforms
@brief Householder QR, forming and applying Q, and batched orthogonalisation.

Lower-level functions (algorithm-specific variants, workspace queries, validation and helpers): @ref api_qr_lowlevel.

@defgroup api_eigen Symmetric and Hermitian eigensolvers
@brief syev and its tiers, selected-range syevx, and the iterative eigensolvers.

Lower-level functions (algorithm-specific variants, workspace queries, validation and helpers): @ref api_eigen_lowlevel.

@defgroup api_tridiag Tridiagonal and band reduction
@brief Reduction to tridiagonal form and the tridiagonal eigensolvers (steqr, stedc, stebz, stein).

Lower-level functions (algorithm-specific variants, workspace queries, validation and helpers): @ref api_tridiag_lowlevel.

@defgroup api_svd Singular value decomposition
@brief gesvd and the bidiagonal pipeline stages.

Lower-level functions (algorithm-specific variants, workspace queries, validation and helpers): @ref api_svd_lowlevel.

@defgroup api_sparse Sparse (CSR) operations
@brief Sparse-dense products, ILU(k) and CSR test-matrix generators.

Lower-level functions (algorithm-specific variants, workspace queries, validation and helpers): @ref api_sparse_lowlevel.

@defgroup api_extra Norms, condition numbers and utilities
@brief Matrix norms, condition estimates, inversion and scaling helpers.

Lower-level functions (algorithm-specific variants, workspace queries, validation and helpers): @ref api_extra_lowlevel.

@defgroup api_linalg Convenience layer (batchlas::linalg)
@brief Value-returning, NumPy-like wrappers over the entry points.

@defgroup api_dispatch Backend dispatch and vendor seams
@brief How a call reaches a backend: backend deduction from the Queue, the USM pointer check, and
the `backend::*_vendor` declarations the vendor families call.

Which kernel runs for a shape is decided by flat kernel selection (developer docs:
@ref api_selection; design: @ref design_flat_selection; per-op families and tables:
@ref selection_tables). Measurements behind each op's choices: @ref perf_evidence.

@defgroup api_workspace Workspace and memory
@brief The per-Queue workspace arena, leases and BumpAllocator sizing.

@defgroup api_errors Errors and convergence status
@brief The exception hierarchy and per-item info reporting.

@defgroup api_config Configuration, environment and tuning
@brief Runtime settings captured from the environment and the tuning constants.

Lower-level functions (algorithm-specific variants, workspace queries, validation and helpers): @ref api_config_lowlevel.

@defgroup api_device In-kernel BLAS and LAPACK (batchlas::device)
@brief BLAS and LAPACK you call from inside your own SYCL kernel, one matrix per work-item,
sub-group or work-group, in namespace batchlas::device.

The device API is header-only: it needs a SYCL compiler and the `include/` directory, not the
built library (see "The device API without building BatchLAS" in @ref guide_cpp_api). A kernel
picks an *executor* (who cooperates on one matrix), wraps its memory in *operands* (typed int32
views), and calls routines whose sizes, layouts and algorithms are template parameters, so a
kernel compiles only the path it takes. LAPACK-level routines run in a *tier* (`tier::Reg`,
`tier::Cta` or `tier::Blocked`) chosen on the host by `with_tier`. The host sizes one matrix's
workspace for a routine tag `R` with `launch<R, Tier, T>::buffer_size(extents...)` or the
equivalent `device::buffer_size<R, Tier, T>(extents...)`; there is no per-op sizer function. A
LAPACK-level routine returns `int info`, the same on every work-item of the executor: 0 on
success and `k > 0` with the routine's LAPACK meaning. The negative values, where a routine
checks its arguments at all, do not follow one numbering across the API: each LAPACK group below
says which rule its routines follow, and each routine lists its values under "Return values".

@par Which header to include
- `#include <batchlas/device.hh>`: the core. Launch, tiers and workspace; executors; operands,
  views and interop; scalars and arithmetic; level 1, level 2, register-tier level 3 and the
  LAPACK auxiliaries.
- `#include <batchlas/device/lapack.hh>`: every LAPACK routine (it includes the core), or one
  routine with `<batchlas/device/lapack/<routine>.hh>`, e.g. `<batchlas/device/lapack/potrf.hh>`.
- `#include <batchlas/device/level3/group.hh>`: in-group level 3 (gemm and friends on one matrix
  per work-group, operands in global memory). `device.hh` does not include it; `lapack.hh` does,
  through the LAPACK headers built on it (`potrf.hh`, `getrf.hh`, `sytrd.hh` and others).
- `#include <batchlas/device/sparse.hh>`: CSR operands, spmm and ILU(k). Neither umbrella
  includes it.

@par What it contains
- @ref api_device_launch &ndash; turning a routine and a tier into a kernel launch.
- @ref api_device_exec &ndash; executors, the work-item teams that own one matrix.
- @ref api_device_operands &ndash; matrices, vectors, fragments, lazy views and host-view interop.
- @ref api_device_arith &ndash; scalar kernels and the arithmetic policies.
- @ref api_device_blas1, @ref api_device_blas2, @ref api_device_blas3 &ndash; BLAS levels 1 to 3.
- LAPACK:
  - @ref api_device_lapack_chol
  - @ref api_device_lapack_lu
  - @ref api_device_lapack_ldl
  - @ref api_device_lapack_qr
  - @ref api_device_lapack_eig
  - @ref api_device_lapack_svd
  - @ref api_device_lapack_aux
- @ref api_device_sparse &ndash; sparse-dense products and incomplete LU.
- @ref api_device_lowlevel &ndash; helpers in batchlas::device that are **not** covered by the
  compatibility promise.

@par Where to read more
- @ref guide_device_api &ndash; the guide: the launch frame, the conventions, the workspace
  protocol, info values and a compiled example per family.
- @ref design_device_la_api &ndash; every signature, by group and header, with its brief
  (generated).
- @ref design_device_la &ndash; the design record: why the API looks the way it does.

@par Compatibility
The semantic-versioning promise ("Versioning and compatibility" in @ref guide_cpp_api) covers
the members of the child groups above except @ref api_device_lowlevel. Anything in
batchlas::device that is in none of them, and everything in `batchlas::device::detail` and
`<batchlas/device/detail/...>`, is not API.

@defgroup api_internal_helpers Kernel helpers (installed, not API)
@brief Headers installed because public headers include them. Not a stable interface.

@}
*/

/**
@namespace batchlas::device
@brief In-kernel BLAS and LAPACK for user SYCL kernels; the reference is @ref api_device.
*/

/**
@defgroup api_device_launch Launch, tiers and workspace
@ingroup api_device
@brief Turning a routine and a tier into a kernel launch: `launch`, `with_tier`, the tiers, the
workspace plan and the per-item slots.

A LAPACK-level routine runs in one tier, fixed at compile time: `tier::Reg` (the matrix in
registers), `tier::Cta` (in local memory) or `tier::Blocked` (any n, blocked through global
workspace). On the host, `with_tier` visits the first tier the device admits for a run-time n, and
`launch<Routine, Tier, T>` gives that tier's buffer size, nd-range, executor, batch slot and
workspace slices, so the host sizes exactly what the kernel carves. Include
`<batchlas/device.hh>` (`tier.hh`, `launch.hh`, `workspace.hh`). Guide: @ref guide_device_api.
*/

/**
@defgroup api_device_exec Executors
@ingroup api_device
@brief The work-item teams that own one matrix: a single work-item, a sub-group or a partition of
one, a work-group, and the batched 2-D grid.

An executor's size is a type constant, so strides, trip counts and team counts fold at compile
time, and every routine distributes its work over the executor's cyclic layout; only `WorkGroup`
has a work-group barrier. Include `<batchlas/device.hh>` (`exec.hh`, `sg_partition.hh`,
`grid.hh`). Guide: @ref guide_device_api.
*/

/**
@defgroup api_device_operands Operands, views and interop
@ingroup api_device
@brief `Mat`, `Vec` and `Frag` operands, the extent, memory-space and layout tags they carry,
lazy views over them, and conversion from host views.

Operands are thin int32 views whose extents, strides, memory space and layout are types: a `Dyn`
extent costs one int, an `Ext<N>` extent is free. Views (`op_view`, `herm_view`, `sym_view`,
rows, columns and slices) compute an index and copy nothing. Interop re-types a host
batchlas::KernelMatrixView or batchlas::VectorView for one batch item. Include `<batchlas/device.hh>` (`core.hh`, `operand.hh`,
`view.hh`, `interop.hh`). Guide: @ref guide_device_api.
*/

/**
@defgroup api_device_arith Scalars and arithmetic
@ingroup api_device
@brief The `Arith` trait, the `arith::` policies a routine's inner loop takes, and the scalar
LAPACK kernels (`lartg`, `safmin` and friends).

`Arith` covers real types, `std::complex` and any plain `{re, im}` complex; the policies choose
explicit or native complex arithmetic and fused multiply-add. The scalar kernels are pure
functions with no executor and are also callable on the host. Include `<batchlas/device.hh>`
(`arith.hh`, `scalar.hh`). Guide: @ref guide_device_api.
*/

/**
@defgroup api_device_blas1 BLAS level 1 and reductions
@ingroup api_device
@brief Vector operations on an executor (copy, swap, fill, axpy, axpby, dot, dotc, sumsq, iamax
and friends) and the reductions and lane exchanges they are built from.

Every operation distributes over the executor's cyclic layout, so two operations on the same
executor compose with no barrier; the only barrier is in the packed scratch exchange, which names
it in its signature. Include `<batchlas/device.hh>` (`level1.hh`, `reduce.hh`). Guide:
@ref guide_device_api.
*/

/**
@defgroup api_device_blas2 BLAS level 2
@ingroup api_device
@brief Matrix-vector operations on an executor: gemv, gbmv, symv / hemv, trmv, trsv, dgmm, and
the rank updates ger, geru, gerc and her2.

Arguments come in BLAS order, `(ex, alpha, A, x, beta, y)`; the algorithm is a template parameter
whose default is computed from the executor and the transpose, so a kernel never compiles the path
it does not take. Include `<batchlas/device.hh>` (`level2.hh`). Guide: @ref guide_device_api.
*/

/**
@defgroup api_device_blas3 BLAS level 3
@ingroup api_device
@brief Matrix-matrix operations: the register-tier gemm, gemm_sub and trsm on one work-item's
tiles, and the in-group gemm, symm / hemm, syrk / herk, syr2k / her2k, trmm_out and trsm on one
matrix per work-group.

The register tier keeps whole operands in registers. The in-group forms take operands in global
memory, stage tiles in the local memory at the start of the workspace, take the host's run-time
modes as template parameters and are tuned through an `l3::` configuration. Include
`<batchlas/device.hh>` for the register tier (`level3.hh`) and
`<batchlas/device/level3/group.hh>` for the in-group forms. Guide: @ref guide_device_api.
*/

/**
@defgroup api_device_lapack_chol Cholesky: potrf, potrs, posv, potri, trtri, lauum
@ingroup api_device
@brief Cholesky factorization of a symmetric or Hermitian positive definite matrix, the solve with
its factor, the combined driver, the inverse from the factor, and the triangular inverse and
product (`trtri`, `lauum`) that the inverse is built from.

Negative `info` values count the arguments from the executor: `ex` is argument 1, so -2 is `A`.
`potrf` returns 0 on success, `k > 0` when the leading minor of order `k` is not positive
definite, -2 when `A` is not square or its order is out of the tier's range, and -3 when the
workspace is missing or misaligned. `potrs` and `posv` return -2 for `A`, -3 for `B` and -4 for
the workspace; `potri`, `trtri` and `lauum` return -2 for `A` and -3 for the workspace, and
`potri` and `trtri` return `i > 0` for an exactly zero diagonal entry. Include
`<batchlas/device/lapack/potrf.hh>` (or `potrs.hh`, `posv.hh`, and `potri.hh` for `potri`,
`trtri` and `lauum`), or `<batchlas/device/lapack.hh>` for all routines. Guide: @ref guide_device_api.
*/

/**
@defgroup api_device_lapack_lu LU: getrf, getrs, gesv, getri, inv
@ingroup api_device
@brief LU factorization with partial pivoting, the solve with its factors, the combined driver,
the inverse from the factors, and the copy-preserving inverse `inv`.

Pivots are 1-based int32, as in LAPACK. These routines check no arguments and return no
negative `info`: `getrf`, `gesv`, `getri` and `inv` return 0 or `k > 0` for the first exactly
zero \f$U_{kk}\f$, and `getrs` always returns 0 (the host getrs has no info). Include
`<batchlas/device/lapack/getrf.hh>` (or `getrs.hh`, `gesv.hh`, `getri.hh`, `inv.hh`), or
`<batchlas/device/lapack.hh>`. Guide: @ref guide_device_api.
*/

/**
@defgroup api_device_lapack_ldl Symmetric indefinite: sytrf, sytrs, sysv
@ingroup api_device
@brief Bunch-Kaufman \f$ A = L D L^T \f$ factorization of a symmetric indefinite matrix, the solve
with its factors and the combined driver.

`info` is LAPACK's: 0, or `k > 0` when \f$D(k, k)\f$ is exactly zero. The negative values are
LAPACK's own argument numbers for xSYTRF, xSYTRS and xSYSV, not positions in the device
signature: -1 for an executor width that is not whole 32-wide sub-groups, -2 for `A` not square
or n outside the tier, -4 (`sytrf`) or -5 (`sytrs`, `sysv`) for `A`'s leading dimension, and, in
`sytrs` and `sysv`, -7 for `B` without n rows and -8 for `B`'s leading dimension. Include
`<batchlas/device/lapack/sytrf.hh>` (or `sytrs.hh`, `sysv.hh`), or
`<batchlas/device/lapack.hh>`. Guide: @ref guide_device_api.
*/

/**
@defgroup api_device_lapack_qr QR and orthogonal transforms: geqrf, larft, larfb, orgqr, ormqr, gebrd, ormbr, ortho
@ingroup api_device
@brief Householder QR, the block reflector and its application, forming and applying Q,
bidiagonal reduction and applying its transforms, and orthogonalisation (`ortho`).

Negative `info` values count the arguments from the first matrix: `A` is argument 1 and `ex` is
not counted. `geqrf` returns -2 for a short `tau` and -3 for a null global workspace; `orgqr` -1
for m < n, and -2 and -3 likewise; `ormqr` -1 when `A`'s rows are not Q's order, -2 for `tau` and
-4 for the workspace; `gebrd` -1 for m < n; `ormbr` -1 for `A` and -3 for `C`. A negative value
means nothing was written. `larft` and `larfb` return nothing. `ortho` does not follow the rule:
it returns -2 when k exceeds the `tier::Cta<N>` bound, and `k > 0` (`potrf`'s info) from its
Cholesky algorithms. The unblocked panels (`geqr2`, `gebd2`, `labrd` and the rest) are in
@ref api_device_lowlevel. Include `<batchlas/device/lapack/geqrf.hh>` (or `larft.hh`, `larfb.hh`, `orgqr.hh`, `ormqr.hh`,
`gebrd.hh`, `ormbr.hh`, `ortho.hh`), or `<batchlas/device/lapack.hh>`. Guide:
@ref guide_device_api.
*/

/**
@defgroup api_device_lapack_eig Symmetric eigensolvers: syev, sytrd, sy2sb, sb2st, steqr, stedc, stebz, stein, sygst, ritz_values
@ingroup api_device
@brief The symmetric and Hermitian eigensolver driver and its stages: tridiagonal and band
reduction, the tridiagonal solvers, the generalized-problem reduction and Rayleigh quotients.

`syev` computes all eigenvalues (and optionally eigenvectors) and reports non-convergence as
`k > 0`. The stages (`sytrd`, `sy2sb` then `sb2st`; `steqr`, `stedc`, `stebz` with `stein`) can
be called on their own. `sygst` reduces the generalized problem to standard form, and
`ritz_values` (`ritz_values.hh`) gives the Rayleigh quotients of the columns of `V`. There is no
device `syevx` or Jacobi solver: `syevx.hh` and `jacobi.hh` hold only pieces of the host routes
(@ref api_device_lowlevel). Negative `info` values count from the executor in `syev`, `sytrd`,
`steqr` and `sygst` (-2 is `A`, or n out of range; `sygst`'s -3 is `B`) and from the first
matrix in `sy2sb` and `sb2st` (-1 is the matrix); `stebz`, `stein`, `stedc` and `ritz_values`
return no negative value. The kernels `stedc` and `stein` are built from (`laed2`, `laed3`,
`laed4`, `lagtf`, `lagts`) are in @ref api_device_lowlevel. Include
`<batchlas/device/lapack/syev.hh>` (or the stage's header, e.g. `steqr.hh`), or
`<batchlas/device/lapack.hh>`. Guide: @ref guide_device_api.
*/

/**
@defgroup api_device_lapack_svd Singular value decomposition: gesvd, bdsqr, bdsdc
@ingroup api_device
@brief The SVD driver and the bidiagonal singular value solvers it runs after `gebrd`.

`gesvd` reduces to bidiagonal form, solves with `bdsqr` (QR iteration) or `bdsdc` (divide and
conquer), and applies the transforms with `ormbr`; non-convergence is reported as `info` 1.
`gesvd`'s negative values count from `A` as argument 1: -2 for a short `S`, and -3 and -4 for a
`U` or `Vh` of the wrong shape. `bdsqr` and `bdsdc` return 0 or 1. Include
`<batchlas/device/lapack/gesvd.hh>` (or `bdsqr.hh`, `bdsdc.hh`), or
`<batchlas/device/lapack.hh>`. Guide: @ref guide_device_api.
*/

/**
@defgroup api_device_lapack_aux LAPACK auxiliaries
@ingroup api_device
@brief The building blocks LAPACK routines share: copies and fills (lacpy, laset, mirror,
trttp / tpttr, geam), norms and scaling (lange, lanst, lascl, laqge, lacgv), row and column
permutations (laswp, lapmr), plane rotations (rot, lasr, largv, lartv, lar2v) and Householder
reflectors (larfg, larfg_lanes, larf).

They run on an executor with its cyclic layout, so they compose with level 1 and level 2
operations on the same executor without a barrier. Include `<batchlas/device.hh>`
(`lapack_aux.hh`). Guide: @ref guide_device_api.
*/

/**
@defgroup api_device_sparse Sparse: spmm, iluk
@ingroup api_device
@brief CSR operands, the sparse-dense product `spmm`, and incomplete LU, `iluk_factorize` and
`iluk_apply`.

Each operation runs one batch item on one executor for any extent; the host sparse routes launch
the same bodies. Include `<batchlas/device/sparse.hh>`, which neither umbrella includes. Guide:
@ref guide_device_api.
*/

/**
@defgroup api_device_lowlevel Low-level helpers (not covered)
@ingroup api_device
@brief Helpers that live in batchlas::device because public routines and the examples use them.
They are **not** covered by the compatibility promise.

A member of this group may change its signature or disappear in any release, minor or patch
("Versioning and compatibility" in @ref guide_cpp_api). They are documented so that the public
routines' signatures can be read; do not build on them. Among them are the divide-and-conquer
merge kernels `laed2`, `laed3` and `laed4` and the inverse-iteration pair `lagtf` and `lagts`
(`<batchlas/device/lapack/laed2.hh>` and siblings, all included by `lapack.hh`): `stedc`, `stein`
and the host kernels share their bodies, and their signatures follow those callers rather than
the device conventions. The rest fall in four sets:
- The tuned-gemm components under `<batchlas/device/level3/...>`: the asynchronous copies
  (`copy_async.hh`), the epilogues and masks (`epilogue.hh`), the MMA slabs (`mma.hh`), the packet
  loads (`packet.hh`), the k-loop pipelines (`pipeline.hh`), the tile schedules (`schedule.hh`)
  and the local-memory staging (`stage.hh`), with `run_tile`, `l3::Pass` and
  `l3::OperandAccessor`.
- The QR and SVD panel leaves: `geqr2`, `geqr2_reg`, `gebd2`, `labrd`, `larfb_from_panel`,
  `larfb_cfg_t` and `larfb_default_nb`.
- The solver panel leaves: `potf2` and its variants, `getf2` and its variants, `sytf2`, `lasyf`,
  `syconv`, `sytrs_col`, `sygs2_group`, `sytd2`, `org2l` and `orm2l`, and the eigensolver steps
  (`laebz_bisect`, `laebz_count`, `stebz_gershgorin`, the `sb2st` chases and
  `ritz_values_quotient`).
- Register, tile, sparse-row and arithmetic helpers that the routines above share, and the
  headers `jacobi.hh` and `syevx.hh`, which hold pieces of host routes and declare no device
  routine.

Everything in `batchlas::device::detail` is private and is not documented at all. Guide:
@ref guide_device_api.
*/

/**
@defgroup api_selection Kernel selection (developer)
@brief How an op picks the kernel that runs: kernel families, per-device tuned tables,
`select::choose`, pins, the trace, the coverage instrument and the vendor seam.

This is library-internal code under `src/select/` and `src/ops/<op>/`; none of it is installed.
The design and its rules R1-R8 are on @ref design_flat_selection. Every op's families and shipped tables are listed on the generated
@ref selection_tables page, which is the authoritative inventory; this group documents the
mechanism they share.

@section selection_one_decision One decision per op, in one file

A public entry point decides what runs in `src/ops/<op>/<op>.cc` and nowhere else. It validates
its arguments, builds a table key from the call, and hands the op's candidates and its `can_run`
predicate to `select::run()`, which picks one choice, opens the trace and coverage scope, and calls
the op's `launch` visitor with it. The op's `<op>_buffer_size` asks `select::pick()` the same
question with the same key, so the workspace query and the call can never size for different
kernels (rule R5).

@section selection_families Families, choices and spellings

A *family* is one kernel driver, written as a small struct: a static `name`, a static `fields`
array naming its integer knobs, `values()`, `from()` and `operator==`. A family with no knobs
derives from `select::NoFields`. An op's *choice* type is a `std::variant` of its families, so a
choice is a value, and it has one spelling everywhere (`family[:field=value]...`, e.g.
`lpanel:panel=8`): in tables, pins, trace lines, coverage rows and test names
(`select::to_string()`, `select::parse()`). Anything a driver derives from the shape and the
device stays derived inside the driver and is never a field.

The op's `choice.hh` lists every compiled choice once in `candidates<T>()`. That list is what the
tuner times, what a pin must name and what a table row may contain, and its order is the tie-break
order: simpler kernels first and native kernels before `vendor`, so near-ties go to the native
kernel. A field-less choice lists its families in variant order (`select::all_of()`).

@section selection_opspec What an op declares once: OpSpec

`select::OpSpec` holds the op's `Op` enumerator (its name in tables, pins and coverage), the vendor
library group its `vendor` family calls (`select::Lib`), and its `select::Rules`: the last-resort
order, by generality, used when no table row names a runnable choice. The default is `blocked`,
then `vendor`, then the rest in candidate order.

@section selection_can_run can_run is correctness only

`can_run(choice, device)` is false exactly when that kernel would throw or give a wrong answer for
the call (rule R3); it never means "slower". Speed lives only in the tables (R4). A speed
threshold in `can_run` would make a pinned kernel unreachable and leave a shape with nothing to run
in a vendor-free build. Each clause should be the argument check at the top of the driver it
guards, and every candidate is pinned in a test on shapes that straddle its limits (R7).

@section selection_table_format Tables, lookup and borrowing

One plain-text file per (op, device), `tuned/<op>.<device>.txt`, one row set per dtype, embedded at build
time and parsed once; a directory named by `BATCHLAS_TUNED_DIR` replaces a built-in table of the
same name. The header names the keys (`# keys: uplo:exact n:log:3 batch:log`); each row is one
measured shape followed by every candidate timed there, fastest first. Inventory and provenance:
@ref tuned_tables_readme.

- **Nearest row** (`select::Table::nearest()`): the rows matching the longest prefix of the
  `:exact` keys, then the smallest weighted \f$ \sum_i w_i\,|\log_2(\mathrm{row}_i/\mathrm{key}_i)| \f$
  over the `:log` keys.
- **Borrow order** (`select::tables_in_borrow_order()`): the device's own table; then tables of the
  same family, nearest architecture at or below first; then other families. A device with no table
  borrows and warns once per op (R8). A CPU device never borrows a GPU table.
- **The walk**: in each table in borrow order, the first entry of the nearest row that is a
  candidate and passes `can_run` wins. If none does anywhere, the last-resort order decides; if
  nothing can run at all, `choose()` throws.

@section selection_pins Pins

`BATCHLAS_<OP>_ROUTE` is consulted on every call, from the settings snapshot (a raw `::setenv` is
not seen until `detail::reload_settings()`; `ScopedEnvVar` triggers one). The value is trimmed and
lower-cased, then means `auto`, `native` (the best runnable non-vendor choice), `vendor`, or a
spelling. A spelling that does not parse, is not a compiled candidate, or
fails `can_run` throws batchlas::invalid_argument (R6); `native` and `vendor` fall back to the
automatic choice with a one-time warning when nothing in their class can run. Tests pin with the
RAII `select::ScopedPin`, which wins over the environment on its thread.

@section selection_trace_coverage Trace and coverage

`BATCHLAS_SELECT_TRACE=1` prints one stderr line per decision, indented under its parent op, with
a tag naming the table used (`[sm_120]`, a borrowed table, `override`, `fallthrough`,
`last resort`, `pinned`). `BATCHLAS_COVERAGE_OUT=<path>` makes `batchlas::coverage` write one CSV
per process: `linked` rows (what this build contains), `reached` rows (what calls actually ran,
with the choice spelling) and `miss` rows (calls nothing could serve). A `linked` row is never
evidence that a kernel runs. Instrument details and defects: the "Coverage instrument" section
of @ref perf_dispatch.

@section selection_vendor_seam The vendor seam

`select::has_library<B>(lib)` is a compile-time answer to "is this op's vendor library in this
build for backend B", and it sets `select::Device::has_vendor`, which is all a `vendor` family's
`can_run` reads. The op's `vendor` arm is an `if constexpr` on it, so with the library absent the
vendor call is not compiled at all and `select::no_vendor()` records a coverage `miss` and throws
`NoRouteError` instead. That is what keeps `-DBATCHLAS_ENABLE_VENDOR_BLAS=OFF` an ordinary build;
see @ref design_vendor_independence.

@section selection_add_op Adding an op

Write `src/ops/<op>/choice.hh` (families, `candidates<T>()`, `OpSpec`, key names, tuner grid) and
`src/ops/<op>/<op>.cc` (key, `can_run`, `launch`, workspace, the public entry point and its
`*_buffer_size`, the instantiations); add tables for every dtype on every shipped device; give the
tuner a spec in `tools/tune/<op>_spec.cc`; pin every candidate in a test. The step-by-step version
is in @ref dev_extending. An op directory without a `choice.hh` still selects by hand; the
generated @ref selection_tables page lists exactly the ops that have one.
*/

/**
@defgroup api_selection_ops Per-op choices
@ingroup api_selection
@brief The vocabulary header of every op under `src/ops/<op>/`: its families, `candidates<T>()`,
`OpSpec`, table keys and tuner grid.

Each `src/ops/<op>/choice.hh` adds itself to this group. Which families rank first on which device,
and where each table came from (measured, converted or transcribed), is on the generated
@ref selection_tables page, and the measurements behind each op's families are on its
`docs/perf/` evidence page.
*/

